    explanation: "Designing a secure REST API involves multiple layers: (1) Authentication — verify identity using OAuth2/JWT tokens rather than sending credentials on every call; (2) Authorization — enforce role/permission-based access control at the endpoint level (e.g., Spring Security method-level @PreAuthorize); (3) Transport security — enforce HTTPS/TLS everywhere to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks; (4) Input validation — validate and sanitize all inputs to prevent injection attacks (SQL injection, XSS); (5) Rate limiting and throttling to prevent abuse/DoS; (6) Proper error handling that doesn't leak stack traces or sensitive internals; (7) CORS configuration to control which origins can call the API; (8) Secrets management — never hardcode API keys or DB passwords, use vaults like AWS Secrets Manager or environment-injected secrets; (9) Logging and auditing of security-relevant events without logging sensitive data like passwords or tokens; (10) Versioning to allow safe evolution of contracts.",
    sampleAnswer: "Designing a secure REST API involves multiple layers: (1) Authentication — verify identity using OAuth2/JWT tokens rather than sending credentials on every call; (2) Authorization — enforce role/permission-based access control at the endpoint level (e.g., Spring Security method-level @PreAuthorize); (3) Transport security — enforce HTTPS/TLS everywhere to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks; (4) Input validation — validate and sanitize all inputs to prevent injection attacks (SQL injection, XSS); (5) Rate limiting and throttling to prevent abuse/DoS; (6) Proper error handling that doesn't leak stack traces or sensitive internals; (7) CORS configuration to control which origins can call the API; (8) Secrets management — never hardcode API keys or DB passwords, use vaults like AWS Secrets Manager or environment-injected secrets; (9) Logging and auditing of security-relevant events without logging sensitive data like passwords or tokens; (10) Versioning to allow safe evolution of contracts.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: JPMorgan Chase\n\nAnswer:\nDesigning a secure REST API involves multiple layers: (1) Authentication — verify identity using OAuth2/JWT tokens rather than sending credentials on every call; (2) Authorization — enforce role/permission-based access control at the endpoint level (e.g., Spring Security method-level @PreAuthorize); (3) Transport security — enforce HTTPS/TLS everywhere to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks; (4) Input validation — validate and sanitize all inputs to prevent injection attacks (SQL injection, XSS); (5) Rate limiting and throttling to prevent abuse/DoS; (6) Proper error handling that doesn't leak stack traces or sensitive internals; (7) CORS configuration to control which origins can call the API; (8) Secrets management — never hardcode API keys or DB passwords, use vaults like AWS Secrets Manager or environment-injected secrets; (9) Logging and auditing of security-relevant events without logging sensitive data like passwords or tokens; (10) Versioning to allow safe evolution of contracts.\n\nExample:\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/api/v1/accounts\")\npublic class AccountController {\n    @PreAuthorize(\"hasRole('ADMIN')\") // authorization\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    public ResponseEntity<Account> getAccount(@PathVariable @Positive Long id) { // input validation\n        // JWT filter already validated authentication before reaching here\n        return ResponseEntity.ok(accountService.findById(id));\n    }\n}\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this design / scenario question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate / Advanced\n\nInterview Focus:\nDesign / Scenario\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
