    question: "What is a daemon thread?",
    slug: "java-what-is-a-daemon-thread-40",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "A daemon thread is a low-priority background thread that runs in service of user (non-daemon) threads and does not prevent the JVM from exiting — once all non-daemon (user) threads finish, the JVM terminates immediately, even if daemon threads are still running, abruptly killing them. This is different from regular (user) threads, where the JVM waits for all of them to complete before shutting down. Examples of daemon threads in the JVM itself include the garbage collector thread. You mark a thread as daemon using setDaemon(true) before calling start().",
    sampleAnswer: "A daemon thread is a low-priority background thread that runs in service of user (non-daemon) threads and does not prevent the JVM from exiting — once all non-daemon (user) threads finish, the JVM terminates immediately, even if daemon threads are still running, abruptly killing them. This is different from regular (user) threads, where the JVM waits for all of them to complete before shutting down. Examples of daemon threads in the JVM itself include the garbage collector thread. You mark a thread as daemon using setDaemon(true) before calling start().",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Volkswagen IT Services\n\nAnswer:\nA daemon thread is a low-priority background thread that runs in service of user (non-daemon) threads and does not prevent the JVM from exiting — once all non-daemon (user) threads finish, the JVM terminates immediately, even if daemon threads are still running, abruptly killing them. This is different from regular (user) threads, where the JVM waits for all of them to complete before shutting down. Examples of daemon threads in the JVM itself include the garbage collector thread. You mark a thread as daemon using setDaemon(true) before calling start().\n\nExample:\nThread backgroundLogger = new Thread(() -> {\n    while (true) {\n        System.out.println(\"Logging heartbeat...\");\n        try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}\n    }\n});\nbackgroundLogger.setDaemon(true); // JVM won't wait for this thread to finish\nbackgroundLogger.start();\n// Main thread finishes its work and exits - JVM terminates, killing backgroundLogger too\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Multithreading & Concurrency, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
