    question: "What does @Entity do?",
    slug: "java-what-does-entity-do-45",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering hibernate & jpa.",
    explanation: "@Entity is a JPA annotation that marks a Java class as a persistent entity — meaning JPA/Hibernate will manage its lifecycle and map it to a corresponding database table. An entity class annotated with @Entity must have a no-argument constructor and a field marked with @Id (the primary key). Once annotated, JPA can generate schema (with tools like Hibernate's hbm2ddl), perform CRUD operations, and manage relationships (via @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, etc.) between this entity and others.",
    sampleAnswer: "@Entity is a JPA annotation that marks a Java class as a persistent entity — meaning JPA/Hibernate will manage its lifecycle and map it to a corresponding database table. An entity class annotated with @Entity must have a no-argument constructor and a field marked with @Id (the primary key). Once annotated, JPA can generate schema (with tools like Hibernate's hbm2ddl), perform CRUD operations, and manage relationships (via @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, etc.) between this entity and others.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Volkswagen IT Services\n\nAnswer:\n@Entity is a JPA annotation that marks a Java class as a persistent entity — meaning JPA/Hibernate will manage its lifecycle and map it to a corresponding database table. An entity class annotated with @Entity must have a no-argument constructor and a field marked with @Id (the primary key). Once annotated, JPA can generate schema (with tools like Hibernate's hbm2ddl), perform CRUD operations, and manage relationships (via @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, etc.) between this entity and others.\n\nExample:\n@Entity\n@Table(name = \"products\")\npublic class Product {\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)\n    private Long id;\n    private String name;\n    private double price;\n    // getters and setters\n}\n// JPA maps this class to a \"products\" table with columns id, name, price\nSECTION 4: Deloitte\n================================================\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Spring / Hibernate / JPA, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
