    question: "Design tables and class relationships for a doctor appointment scheduling system.",
    slug: "java-design-tables-and-class-relationships-for-a-doctor-appointment-scheduling-system-46",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering collections & streams.",
    explanation: "A doctor appointment system typically needs at minimum these entities: Patient (id, name, contact info), Doctor (id, name, specialization), Appointment (id, patient_id FK, doctor_id FK, appointment_time, status), and optionally Slot/Availability (doctor_id FK, date, time_range, is_booked) to track which time slots a doctor offers. The relationships are: a Doctor has many Appointments (one-to-many), a Patient has many Appointments (one-to-many), and Appointment acts as a junction/associative entity connecting Doctor and Patient (effectively representing a many-to-many relationship between them, enriched with appointment-specific data like time and status). In Java classes, this maps to Doctor and Patient each holding a List<Appointment>, and Appointment holding references to a single Doctor and single Patient.",
    sampleAnswer: "A doctor appointment system typically needs at minimum these entities: Patient (id, name, contact info), Doctor (id, name, specialization), Appointment (id, patient_id FK, doctor_id FK, appointment_time, status), and optionally Slot/Availability (doctor_id FK, date, time_range, is_booked) to track which time slots a doctor offers. The relationships are: a Doctor has many Appointments (one-to-many), a Patient has many Appointments (one-to-many), and Appointment acts as a junction/associative entity connecting Doctor and Patient (effectively representing a many-to-many relationship between them, enriched with appointment-specific data like time and status). In Java classes, this maps to Doctor and Patient each holding a List<Appointment>, and Appointment holding references to a single Doctor and single Patient.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Deloitte\n\nAnswer:\nA doctor appointment system typically needs at minimum these entities: Patient (id, name, contact info), Doctor (id, name, specialization), Appointment (id, patient_id FK, doctor_id FK, appointment_time, status), and optionally Slot/Availability (doctor_id FK, date, time_range, is_booked) to track which time slots a doctor offers. The relationships are: a Doctor has many Appointments (one-to-many), a Patient has many Appointments (one-to-many), and Appointment acts as a junction/associative entity connecting Doctor and Patient (effectively representing a many-to-many relationship between them, enriched with appointment-specific data like time and status). In Java classes, this maps to Doctor and Patient each holding a List<Appointment>, and Appointment holding references to a single Doctor and single Patient.\n\nExample:\nCREATE TABLE doctor (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100), specialization VARCHAR(50));\nCREATE TABLE patient (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100), phone VARCHAR(20));\nCREATE TABLE appointment (\n    id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    doctor_id INT REFERENCES doctor(id),\n    patient_id INT REFERENCES patient(id),\n    appointment_time DATETIME,\n    status VARCHAR(20) -- SCHEDULED, COMPLETED, CANCELLED\n);\n\n@Entity class Doctor { @Id Long id; String name; @OneToMany(mappedBy=\"doctor\") List<Appointment> appointments; }\n@Entity class Patient { @Id Long id; String name; @OneToMany(mappedBy=\"patient\") List<Appointment> appointments; }\n@Entity class Appointment { @Id Long id; @ManyToOne Doctor doctor; @ManyToOne Patient patient; LocalDateTime time; String status; }\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this design / scenario question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate / Advanced\n\nInterview Focus:\nDesign / Scenario\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
