    question: "Find the number of occurrences of a pattern in a circular linked list.",
    slug: "java-find-the-number-of-occurrences-of-a-pattern-in-a-circular-linked-list-50",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering core java.",
    explanation: "A circular linked list is one where the last node points back to the first node instead of null. To find occurrences of a pattern (a sequence of values), you traverse the list starting from the head, comparing consecutive nodes against the pattern sequence, and because the list is circular, you must track how many nodes you've visited (or detect when you've returned to the starting node) to avoid infinite looping. For each starting position within one full cycle, you check whether the pattern matches starting there, incrementing a counter on each match.",
    sampleAnswer: "A circular linked list is one where the last node points back to the first node instead of null. To find occurrences of a pattern (a sequence of values), you traverse the list starting from the head, comparing consecutive nodes against the pattern sequence, and because the list is circular, you must track how many nodes you've visited (or detect when you've returned to the starting node) to avoid infinite looping. For each starting position within one full cycle, you check whether the pattern matches starting there, incrementing a counter on each match.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Deloitte\n\nAnswer:\nA circular linked list is one where the last node points back to the first node instead of null. To find occurrences of a pattern (a sequence of values), you traverse the list starting from the head, comparing consecutive nodes against the pattern sequence, and because the list is circular, you must track how many nodes you've visited (or detect when you've returned to the starting node) to avoid infinite looping. For each starting position within one full cycle, you check whether the pattern matches starting there, incrementing a counter on each match.\n\nExample:\npublic static int countPatternOccurrences(Node head, int[] pattern) {\n    if (head == null) return 0;\n    int count = 0;\n    int totalNodes = countNodes(head); // count nodes once to know cycle length\n    Node current = head;\n    for (int i = 0; i < totalNodes; i++) {\n        if (matchesPatternFrom(current, pattern)) count++;\n        current = current.next;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\nprivate static boolean matchesPatternFrom(Node start, int[] pattern) {\n    Node temp = start;\n    for (int val : pattern) {\n        if (temp.data != val) return false;\n        temp = temp.next;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this coding / problem solving question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nCoding / Problem Solving\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
