    question: "Solve a string-permutation coding problem.",
    slug: "java-solve-a-string-permutation-coding-problem-73",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering core java.",
    explanation: "Generating all permutations of a string is a classic backtracking problem. The idea is to fix one character at a time at the current position, recursively permute the remaining characters, then swap back (backtrack) to try the next character at that position. For a string of length n, there are n! permutations, so this approach has exponential time complexity, which is expected for this class of problem.",
    sampleAnswer: "Generating all permutations of a string is a classic backtracking problem. The idea is to fix one character at a time at the current position, recursively permute the remaining characters, then swap back (backtrack) to try the next character at that position. For a string of length n, there are n! permutations, so this approach has exponential time complexity, which is expected for this class of problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Maybank\n\nAnswer:\nGenerating all permutations of a string is a classic backtracking problem. The idea is to fix one character at a time at the current position, recursively permute the remaining characters, then swap back (backtrack) to try the next character at that position. For a string of length n, there are n! permutations, so this approach has exponential time complexity, which is expected for this class of problem.\n\nExample:\npublic static void permute(char[] arr, int start) {\n    if (start == arr.length - 1) {\n        System.out.println(new String(arr));\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int i = start; i < arr.length; i++) {\n        swap(arr, start, i);\n        permute(arr, start + 1); // recurse on the rest\n        swap(arr, start, i); // backtrack\n    }\n}\nprivate static void swap(char[] arr, int i, int j) {\n    char temp = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = temp;\n}\n// permute(\"ABC\".toCharArray(), 0) prints: ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CBA, CAB\n\n\nSECTION 8: Finzly\n================================================\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
