    explanation: "HTTP status codes are grouped by their first digit. 2xx (Success): 200 OK (successful GET/PUT), 201 Created (successful POST that created a resource), 204 No Content (successful request with no response body, common for DELETE). 3xx (Redirection): 301 Moved Permanently, 304 Not Modified (used with caching). 4xx (Client Error): 400 Bad Request (malformed request), 401 Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not authorized), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource), 429 Too Many Requests (rate limiting). 5xx (Server Error): 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled exception), 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable (server overloaded or down for maintenance).",
    sampleAnswer: "HTTP status codes are grouped by their first digit. 2xx (Success): 200 OK (successful GET/PUT), 201 Created (successful POST that created a resource), 204 No Content (successful request with no response body, common for DELETE). 3xx (Redirection): 301 Moved Permanently, 304 Not Modified (used with caching). 4xx (Client Error): 400 Bad Request (malformed request), 401 Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not authorized), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource), 429 Too Many Requests (rate limiting). 5xx (Server Error): 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled exception), 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable (server overloaded or down for maintenance).",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: Finzly\n\nAnswer:\nHTTP status codes are grouped by their first digit. 2xx (Success): 200 OK (successful GET/PUT), 201 Created (successful POST that created a resource), 204 No Content (successful request with no response body, common for DELETE). 3xx (Redirection): 301 Moved Permanently, 304 Not Modified (used with caching). 4xx (Client Error): 400 Bad Request (malformed request), 401 Unauthorized (missing/invalid authentication), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not authorized), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource), 429 Too Many Requests (rate limiting). 5xx (Server Error): 500 Internal Server Error (unhandled exception), 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable (server overloaded or down for maintenance).\n\nExample:\n// Returning proper status codes in Spring\n@PostMapping(\"/users\")\npublic ResponseEntity<User> createUser(@RequestBody User user) {\n    User saved = userService.save(user);\n    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(saved); // 201 Created\n}\n@GetMapping(\"/users/{id}\")\npublic ResponseEntity<User> getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return userService.findById(id)\n        .map(ResponseEntity::ok) // 200 OK\n        .orElse(ResponseEntity.notFound().build()); // 404 Not Found\n}\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
