    question: "Compare SQL and NoSQL.",
    slug: "java-compare-sql-and-nosql-79",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering jdbc & databases.",
    explanation: "SQL (relational) databases like PostgreSQL and MySQL store data in structured tables with predefined schemas, enforce ACID transactions, and use SQL for querying with strong support for complex joins and relationships — they're ideal when data integrity, relationships, and consistency are critical (e.g., financial systems). NoSQL databases (like MongoDB, Cassandra, DynamoDB) use flexible schemas (document, key-value, column-family, or graph models), typically prioritize horizontal scalability and high write/read throughput over strict consistency (often following the BASE model and eventual consistency rather than ACID), and are well-suited for unstructured/semi-structured data, rapidly evolving schemas, and massive scale (e.g., social media feeds, IoT data, catalogs).",
    sampleAnswer: "SQL (relational) databases like PostgreSQL and MySQL store data in structured tables with predefined schemas, enforce ACID transactions, and use SQL for querying with strong support for complex joins and relationships — they're ideal when data integrity, relationships, and consistency are critical (e.g., financial systems). NoSQL databases (like MongoDB, Cassandra, DynamoDB) use flexible schemas (document, key-value, column-family, or graph models), typically prioritize horizontal scalability and high write/read throughput over strict consistency (often following the BASE model and eventual consistency rather than ACID), and are well-suited for unstructured/semi-structured data, rapidly evolving schemas, and massive scale (e.g., social media feeds, IoT data, catalogs).",
