    question: "Compare PostgreSQL and MongoDB.",
    slug: "java-compare-postgresql-and-mongodb-80",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering jdbc & databases.",
    explanation: "PostgreSQL is a relational (SQL) database with a fixed schema, strong ACID transaction guarantees, rich support for complex joins, and advanced features like window functions, full-text search, and even native JSONB columns for semi-structured data — making it a versatile choice for most general-purpose applications. MongoDB is a document-oriented NoSQL database that stores data as flexible, schema-less BSON documents, which is well-suited for rapidly evolving data models, nested/hierarchical data, and horizontal scaling (sharding) across many servers, but traditionally offered weaker multi-document transaction guarantees than PostgreSQL (though modern MongoDB versions have added multi-document ACID transactions too). Choose PostgreSQL for structured, relational data with complex queries; choose MongoDB when your data is naturally document-shaped and your schema needs to evolve quickly, or when you need to scale writes horizontally across many nodes easily.",
