    question: "What is Optional and when should it not be used?",
    slug: "java-what-is-optional-and-when-should-it-not-be-used-102",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering collections & streams.",
    explanation: "Optional<T> is a container object introduced in Java 8 that may or may not hold a non-null value, designed to make the possibility of \"absence of a value\" explicit in a method's return type, reducing accidental NullPointerExceptions and forcing callers to consciously handle the absent case (via isPresent(), orElse(), map(), ifPresent(), etc.) rather than silently receiving a null. However, Optional should NOT be used as a field type in a class (it's not Serializable and adds unnecessary wrapping overhead for internal state), should NOT be used as a method parameter type (it complicates the API — better to overload methods or use a default value instead), and should not be used for collections (an empty List is preferable to Optional<List<T>>). Its intended, idiomatic use is specifically as a return type for methods that might not have a result to give back.",
