    question: "Future vs CompletableFuture?",
    slug: "java-future-vs-completablefuture-113",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "Future represents the result of an asynchronous computation but has limited capabilities — you can only check if it's done (isDone()) or block and wait for the result (get()), with no way to chain further actions, combine multiple futures, or be notified/callback when it completes without blocking. CompletableFuture (Java 8+) is a much richer implementation of Future that supports composing and chaining asynchronous operations declaratively (thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine), combining multiple futures, adding callbacks for completion/exception (whenComplete, exceptionally) without blocking, and manually completing a future from outside. It essentially brings a full functional, non-blocking asynchronous programming model to Java.",
    sampleAnswer: "Future represents the result of an asynchronous computation but has limited capabilities — you can only check if it's done (isDone()) or block and wait for the result (get()), with no way to chain further actions, combine multiple futures, or be notified/callback when it completes without blocking. CompletableFuture (Java 8+) is a much richer implementation of Future that supports composing and chaining asynchronous operations declaratively (thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine), combining multiple futures, adding callbacks for completion/exception (whenComplete, exceptionally) without blocking, and manually completing a future from outside. It essentially brings a full functional, non-blocking asynchronous programming model to Java.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - Core Java, Collections, Streams\n\nAnswer:\nFuture represents the result of an asynchronous computation but has limited capabilities — you can only check if it's done (isDone()) or block and wait for the result (get()), with no way to chain further actions, combine multiple futures, or be notified/callback when it completes without blocking. CompletableFuture (Java 8+) is a much richer implementation of Future that supports composing and chaining asynchronous operations declaratively (thenApply, thenCompose, thenCombine), combining multiple futures, adding callbacks for completion/exception (whenComplete, exceptionally) without blocking, and manually completing a future from outside. It essentially brings a full functional, non-blocking asynchronous programming model to Java.\n\nExample:\n// Future - can only block and wait, no chaining\nExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\nFuture<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> computeValue());\nint result = future.get(); // blocks the calling thread until done\n\n// CompletableFuture - can chain non-blocking operations\nCompletableFuture<Integer> cf = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> computeValue())\n    .thenApply(value -> value * 2)         // transform the result once ready\n    .thenApply(value -> value + 10);       // chain another transformation\ncf.thenAccept(result -> System.out.println(\"Final result: \" + result)); // callback, non-blocking\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Multithreading & Concurrency, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
