    question: "What is JDBC and what are the normal steps to execute a query?",
    slug: "java-what-is-jdbc-and-what-are-the-normal-steps-to-execute-a-query-118",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering hibernate & jpa.",
    explanation: "JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that provides a vendor-neutral way to connect to and interact with relational databases using SQL. The normal steps are: (1) Load the database driver (mostly automatic in modern JDBC via ServiceLoader, previously required Class.forName()); (2) Establish a Connection using DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); (3) Create a Statement or PreparedStatement from the connection; (4) Execute the query (executeQuery() for SELECT, executeUpdate() for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); (5) Process the ResultSet if applicable, iterating with next() and extracting column values; (6) Close all resources (ResultSet, Statement, Connection) in reverse order, ideally using try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup even on exceptions.",
    sampleAnswer: "JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that provides a vendor-neutral way to connect to and interact with relational databases using SQL. The normal steps are: (1) Load the database driver (mostly automatic in modern JDBC via ServiceLoader, previously required Class.forName()); (2) Establish a Connection using DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); (3) Create a Statement or PreparedStatement from the connection; (4) Execute the query (executeQuery() for SELECT, executeUpdate() for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); (5) Process the ResultSet if applicable, iterating with next() and extracting column values; (6) Close all resources (ResultSet, Statement, Connection) in reverse order, ideally using try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup even on exceptions.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - JDBC, Spring, Hibernate/JPA, Production\n\nAnswer:\nJDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that provides a vendor-neutral way to connect to and interact with relational databases using SQL. The normal steps are: (1) Load the database driver (mostly automatic in modern JDBC via ServiceLoader, previously required Class.forName()); (2) Establish a Connection using DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password); (3) Create a Statement or PreparedStatement from the connection; (4) Execute the query (executeQuery() for SELECT, executeUpdate() for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); (5) Process the ResultSet if applicable, iterating with next() and extracting column values; (6) Close all resources (ResultSet, Statement, Connection) in reverse order, ideally using try-with-resources to guarantee cleanup even on exceptions.\n\nExample:\nString sql = \"SELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE id = ?\";\ntry (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);\n     PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n    ps.setInt(1, 101);\n    try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n        while (rs.next()) {\n            System.out.println(rs.getString(\"name\") + \": \" + rs.getDouble(\"salary\"));\n        }\n    }\n} // all resources auto-closed here\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Spring / Hibernate / JPA, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
