    question: "Why might @Transactional appear not to work?",
    slug: "java-why-might-transactional-appear-not-to-work-136",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering spring & spring boot.",
    explanation: "@Transactional relies on Spring's proxy-based AOP mechanism, so it silently fails to apply in several common scenarios: (1) Self-invocation — calling an @Transactional method from another method WITHIN the same class (this.someTransactionalMethod()) bypasses the proxy entirely, since the call doesn't go through the Spring-managed proxy object; (2) the method is private (proxies can only intercept public method calls, since the proxy needs to override/wrap the method); (3) the exception thrown is a checked exception without configuring rollbackFor (by default only unchecked/RuntimeExceptions trigger rollback); (4) the class isn't a Spring-managed bean at all (e.g., you instantiated it manually with \"new\" instead of letting Spring inject it); (5) using CGLIB proxies on a final class or final method, which can't be proxied/overridden.",
    sampleAnswer: "@Transactional relies on Spring's proxy-based AOP mechanism, so it silently fails to apply in several common scenarios: (1) Self-invocation — calling an @Transactional method from another method WITHIN the same class (this.someTransactionalMethod()) bypasses the proxy entirely, since the call doesn't go through the Spring-managed proxy object; (2) the method is private (proxies can only intercept public method calls, since the proxy needs to override/wrap the method); (3) the exception thrown is a checked exception without configuring rollbackFor (by default only unchecked/RuntimeExceptions trigger rollback); (4) the class isn't a Spring-managed bean at all (e.g., you instantiated it manually with \"new\" instead of letting Spring inject it); (5) using CGLIB proxies on a final class or final method, which can't be proxied/overridden.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - JDBC, Spring, Hibernate/JPA, Production\n\nAnswer:\n@Transactional relies on Spring's proxy-based AOP mechanism, so it silently fails to apply in several common scenarios: (1) Self-invocation — calling an @Transactional method from another method WITHIN the same class (this.someTransactionalMethod()) bypasses the proxy entirely, since the call doesn't go through the Spring-managed proxy object; (2) the method is private (proxies can only intercept public method calls, since the proxy needs to override/wrap the method); (3) the exception thrown is a checked exception without configuring rollbackFor (by default only unchecked/RuntimeExceptions trigger rollback); (4) the class isn't a Spring-managed bean at all (e.g., you instantiated it manually with \"new\" instead of letting Spring inject it); (5) using CGLIB proxies on a final class or final method, which can't be proxied/overridden.\n\nExample:\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    public void placeOrder(Order order) {\n        this.saveOrder(order); // BUG: self-invocation bypasses the proxy - @Transactional below won't apply!\n    }\n    @Transactional\n    public void saveOrder(Order order) { orderRepo.save(order); }\n}\n// Fix: inject OrderService into itself (via a separate bean/interface) or restructure\n// so the transactional method is called from a DIFFERENT bean, going through the proxy.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Spring / Hibernate / JPA, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
