    question: "How would you test transactional behavior?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-test-transactional-behavior-157",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering spring & spring boot.",
    explanation: "In Spring, you can test that @Transactional methods correctly commit or roll back by writing an integration test (typically @SpringBootTest or @DataJpaTest) that deliberately triggers both success and failure scenarios and asserts on the resulting database state. Spring Test's @Transactional annotation on the TEST method itself wraps each test in its own transaction that's automatically rolled back after the test completes (keeping tests isolated from each other), which is convenient — but if you specifically want to verify that YOUR application code's rollback behavior works correctly (e.g., that an exception mid-method really does undo a partial database change), you often need TestTransaction utilities or a separate, non-rolled-back verification step, since the test's own wrapping transaction can otherwise mask whether your method's internal rollback actually worked.",
    sampleAnswer: "In Spring, you can test that @Transactional methods correctly commit or roll back by writing an integration test (typically @SpringBootTest or @DataJpaTest) that deliberately triggers both success and failure scenarios and asserts on the resulting database state. Spring Test's @Transactional annotation on the TEST method itself wraps each test in its own transaction that's automatically rolled back after the test completes (keeping tests isolated from each other), which is convenient — but if you specifically want to verify that YOUR application code's rollback behavior works correctly (e.g., that an exception mid-method really does undo a partial database change), you often need TestTransaction utilities or a separate, non-rolled-back verification step, since the test's own wrapping transaction can otherwise mask whether your method's internal rollback actually worked.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - JDBC, Spring, Hibernate/JPA, Production\n\nAnswer:\nIn Spring, you can test that @Transactional methods correctly commit or roll back by writing an integration test (typically @SpringBootTest or @DataJpaTest) that deliberately triggers both success and failure scenarios and asserts on the resulting database state. Spring Test's @Transactional annotation on the TEST method itself wraps each test in its own transaction that's automatically rolled back after the test completes (keeping tests isolated from each other), which is convenient — but if you specifically want to verify that YOUR application code's rollback behavior works correctly (e.g., that an exception mid-method really does undo a partial database change), you often need TestTransaction utilities or a separate, non-rolled-back verification step, since the test's own wrapping transaction can otherwise mask whether your method's internal rollback actually worked.\n\nExample:\n@SpringBootTest\nclass BankServiceTransactionTest {\n    @Autowired BankService bankService;\n    @Autowired AccountRepository accountRepo;\n\n    @Test\n    void testTransferRollsBackOnFailure() {\n        double initialBalance = accountRepo.findById(1L).get().getBalance();\n        assertThrows(RuntimeException.class, () -> {\n            bankService.transferMoney(1L, 999L, 100); // 999L doesn't exist - should fail and roll back\n        });\n        double balanceAfter = accountRepo.findById(1L).get().getBalance();\n        assertEquals(initialBalance, balanceAfter); // confirms the debit was rolled back, not partially applied\n    }\n}\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Spring / Hibernate / JPA, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
