    question: "How would you diagnose HikariCP connection timeouts?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-diagnose-hikaricp-connection-timeouts-162",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering spring & spring boot.",
    explanation: "A HikariCP connection timeout (SQLTransientConnectionException: \"Connection is not available, request timed out\") means all connections in the pool were busy and no connection became free within the configured connectionTimeout. Diagnosis: (1) enable HikariCP's leak detection (leakDetectionThreshold) to log a warning with a stack trace when a connection is held longer than expected, revealing code that isn't closing/returning connections properly; (2) check the pool metrics via Actuator/Micrometer (active vs idle vs pending connections) to see if the pool is genuinely saturated under load or if connections are leaking; (3) check for long-running queries or transactions holding connections far longer than necessary (often the real root cause — a slow query or a transaction spanning an external API call); (4) verify pool size is appropriately sized (see HikariCP sizing formula) relative to actual concurrent demand; (5) check for connection leaks in code paths that don't use try-with-resources or that acquire a connection outside the managed Spring transaction boundary.",
    sampleAnswer: "A HikariCP connection timeout (SQLTransientConnectionException: \"Connection is not available, request timed out\") means all connections in the pool were busy and no connection became free within the configured connectionTimeout. Diagnosis: (1) enable HikariCP's leak detection (leakDetectionThreshold) to log a warning with a stack trace when a connection is held longer than expected, revealing code that isn't closing/returning connections properly; (2) check the pool metrics via Actuator/Micrometer (active vs idle vs pending connections) to see if the pool is genuinely saturated under load or if connections are leaking; (3) check for long-running queries or transactions holding connections far longer than necessary (often the real root cause — a slow query or a transaction spanning an external API call); (4) verify pool size is appropriately sized (see HikariCP sizing formula) relative to actual concurrent demand; (5) check for connection leaks in code paths that don't use try-with-resources or that acquire a connection outside the managed Spring transaction boundary.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - Resilience, Locking, Caching, Idempotency\n\nAnswer:\nA HikariCP connection timeout (SQLTransientConnectionException: \"Connection is not available, request timed out\") means all connections in the pool were busy and no connection became free within the configured connectionTimeout. Diagnosis: (1) enable HikariCP's leak detection (leakDetectionThreshold) to log a warning with a stack trace when a connection is held longer than expected, revealing code that isn't closing/returning connections properly; (2) check the pool metrics via Actuator/Micrometer (active vs idle vs pending connections) to see if the pool is genuinely saturated under load or if connections are leaking; (3) check for long-running queries or transactions holding connections far longer than necessary (often the real root cause — a slow query or a transaction spanning an external API call); (4) verify pool size is appropriately sized (see HikariCP sizing formula) relative to actual concurrent demand; (5) check for connection leaks in code paths that don't use try-with-resources or that acquire a connection outside the managed Spring transaction boundary.\n\nExample:\nspring.datasource.hikari.leak-detection-threshold=30000  # warns if a connection is held > 30 seconds\n# Example log output revealing the culprit:\n# \"Connection leak detection triggered for connection ... on thread report-generator-3,\n#  stack trace: com.example.ReportService.generateReport(ReportService.java:45)\"\n// This immediately reveals ReportService is not closing its connection/resultset properly.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
