    question: "How would you make a POST endpoint idempotent when clients retry requests?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-make-a-post-endpoint-idempotent-when-clients-retry-requests-163",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "Idempotency means multiple identical requests produce the same result as a single request (no duplicate side effects like double-charging a customer). A common pattern: require the client to send a unique Idempotency-Key header with each logically-distinct request; on the server, before processing, check if that key has already been seen (stored in a fast store like Redis or a database table with a unique constraint on the key) — if yes, return the previously stored response instead of re-executing the operation; if no, process the request normally and store the key alongside the response for future duplicate checks (with an appropriate expiration).",
    sampleAnswer: "Idempotency means multiple identical requests produce the same result as a single request (no duplicate side effects like double-charging a customer). A common pattern: require the client to send a unique Idempotency-Key header with each logically-distinct request; on the server, before processing, check if that key has already been seen (stored in a fast store like Redis or a database table with a unique constraint on the key) — if yes, return the previously stored response instead of re-executing the operation; if no, process the request normally and store the key alongside the response for future duplicate checks (with an appropriate expiration).",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - Resilience, Locking, Caching, Idempotency\n\nAnswer:\nIdempotency means multiple identical requests produce the same result as a single request (no duplicate side effects like double-charging a customer). A common pattern: require the client to send a unique Idempotency-Key header with each logically-distinct request; on the server, before processing, check if that key has already been seen (stored in a fast store like Redis or a database table with a unique constraint on the key) — if yes, return the previously stored response instead of re-executing the operation; if no, process the request normally and store the key alongside the response for future duplicate checks (with an appropriate expiration).\n\nExample:\n@PostMapping(\"/payments\")\npublic ResponseEntity<PaymentResponse> createPayment(\n        @RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String idempotencyKey,\n        @RequestBody PaymentRequest request) {\n    Optional<PaymentResponse> existing = idempotencyStore.get(idempotencyKey);\n    if (existing.isPresent()) {\n        return ResponseEntity.ok(existing.get()); // return the SAME response, don't re-charge the customer\n    }\n    PaymentResponse response = paymentService.process(request);\n    idempotencyStore.save(idempotencyKey, response, Duration.ofHours(24));\n    return ResponseEntity.ok(response);\n}\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nBeginner / Intermediate\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
