    question: "How would you investigate high CPU in a Java service?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-investigate-high-cpu-in-a-java-service-165",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "(1) Identify which threads are consuming CPU using OS tools (top -H -p <pid> on Linux to see per-thread CPU usage); (2) take a thread dump (jstack <pid>, or via Actuator's /actuator/threaddump) at the moment of high CPU and match the high-CPU thread IDs (converted to hex) against the thread dump to see exactly what code those threads are executing; (3) alternatively, use a CPU profiler (async-profiler, JFR) to capture a flame graph over a time window, which directly shows which methods are consuming the most CPU time without manual thread-ID matching; (4) common root causes include: an infinite/tight loop (e.g., a bug causing a spin-wait instead of blocking), excessive garbage collection due to high allocation rates (visible in GC logs), regex catastrophic backtracking, or inefficient algorithms (like an O(n²) operation on a much-larger-than-expected dataset).",
