    question: "How would you troubleshoot database connection exhaustion?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-troubleshoot-database-connection-exhaustion-167",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "Connection exhaustion (all pool connections in use, new requests time out waiting) is diagnosed by: (1) monitoring pool metrics (active, idle, pending/waiting threads) via Actuator/Micrometer to confirm the pool is indeed maxed out; (2) enabling HikariCP leak detection to catch code that acquires but never releases a connection; (3) checking for long-running transactions or queries that hold a connection far longer than expected (a slow query, or a transaction that makes a slow external API call while still holding a DB connection open, is a very common culprit); (4) reviewing recent traffic patterns for an unexpected surge beyond the pool's designed capacity; (5) checking whether the configured pool size is actually appropriate for the workload (either genuinely too small, or the database server itself can't handle more connections without proportionally increasing pool size elsewhere).",
    sampleAnswer: "Connection exhaustion (all pool connections in use, new requests time out waiting) is diagnosed by: (1) monitoring pool metrics (active, idle, pending/waiting threads) via Actuator/Micrometer to confirm the pool is indeed maxed out; (2) enabling HikariCP leak detection to catch code that acquires but never releases a connection; (3) checking for long-running transactions or queries that hold a connection far longer than expected (a slow query, or a transaction that makes a slow external API call while still holding a DB connection open, is a very common culprit); (4) reviewing recent traffic patterns for an unexpected surge beyond the pool's designed capacity; (5) checking whether the configured pool size is actually appropriate for the workload (either genuinely too small, or the database server itself can't handle more connections without proportionally increasing pool size elsewhere).",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - Resilience, Locking, Caching, Idempotency\n\nAnswer:\nConnection exhaustion (all pool connections in use, new requests time out waiting) is diagnosed by: (1) monitoring pool metrics (active, idle, pending/waiting threads) via Actuator/Micrometer to confirm the pool is indeed maxed out; (2) enabling HikariCP leak detection to catch code that acquires but never releases a connection; (3) checking for long-running transactions or queries that hold a connection far longer than expected (a slow query, or a transaction that makes a slow external API call while still holding a DB connection open, is a very common culprit); (4) reviewing recent traffic patterns for an unexpected surge beyond the pool's designed capacity; (5) checking whether the configured pool size is actually appropriate for the workload (either genuinely too small, or the database server itself can't handle more connections without proportionally increasing pool size elsewhere).\n\nExample:\n// A classic anti-pattern causing connection exhaustion: holding a DB connection open\n// while making a slow external HTTP call inside the same transaction\n@Transactional\npublic void processOrder(Order order) {\n    orderRepo.save(order); // holds a DB connection\n    shippingApiClient.notifyCarrier(order); // SLOW external call - the DB connection stays checked out\n                                             // from the pool the entire time, unnecessarily\n}\n// Fix: move the external call outside the transactional boundary, or make it asynchronous,\n// so the database connection is released back to the pool as quickly as possible.\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this conceptual question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of SQL / Database, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate / Advanced\n\nInterview Focus:\nConceptual\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
