    question: "How would you design a resilient service calling a slow external API?",
    slug: "java-how-would-you-design-a-resilient-service-calling-a-slow-external-api-173",
    shortDescription: "Java interview question covering concurrency & multithreading.",
    explanation: "Key resilience patterns: (1) Timeouts — always set an explicit connection and read timeout on external calls, never rely on defaults (which can be infinite), to avoid threads hanging forever; (2) Retries with exponential backoff — for transient failures, retry a limited number of times with increasing delay between attempts (and ideally jitter, to avoid synchronized retry storms across many clients); (3) Circuit Breaker — using a library like Resilience4j, if calls to the external service keep failing, \"open\" the circuit to fail fast immediately (without even attempting the call) for a cooldown period, giving the struggling downstream service time to recover, then periodically test with a few \"half-open\" trial calls before fully closing the circuit again; (4) Bulkheads — isolate resources (thread pools) dedicated to a specific external dependency, so a slow/failing dependency can't exhaust shared resources needed by unrelated parts of the application; (5) Fallbacks — provide a sensible default/cached response when the external call ultimately fails, if a degraded experience is acceptable, rather than failing the entire user-facing request.",
    sampleAnswer: "Key resilience patterns: (1) Timeouts — always set an explicit connection and read timeout on external calls, never rely on defaults (which can be infinite), to avoid threads hanging forever; (2) Retries with exponential backoff — for transient failures, retry a limited number of times with increasing delay between attempts (and ideally jitter, to avoid synchronized retry storms across many clients); (3) Circuit Breaker — using a library like Resilience4j, if calls to the external service keep failing, \"open\" the circuit to fail fast immediately (without even attempting the call) for a cooldown period, giving the struggling downstream service time to recover, then periodically test with a few \"half-open\" trial calls before fully closing the circuit again; (4) Bulkheads — isolate resources (thread pools) dedicated to a specific external dependency, so a slow/failing dependency can't exhaust shared resources needed by unrelated parts of the application; (5) Fallbacks — provide a sensible default/cached response when the external call ultimately fails, if a degraded experience is acceptable, rather than failing the entire user-facing request.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source section: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY QUESTIONS - Resilience, Locking, Caching, Idempotency\n\nAnswer:\nKey resilience patterns: (1) Timeouts — always set an explicit connection and read timeout on external calls, never rely on defaults (which can be infinite), to avoid threads hanging forever; (2) Retries with exponential backoff — for transient failures, retry a limited number of times with increasing delay between attempts (and ideally jitter, to avoid synchronized retry storms across many clients); (3) Circuit Breaker — using a library like Resilience4j, if calls to the external service keep failing, \"open\" the circuit to fail fast immediately (without even attempting the call) for a cooldown period, giving the struggling downstream service time to recover, then periodically test with a few \"half-open\" trial calls before fully closing the circuit again; (4) Bulkheads — isolate resources (thread pools) dedicated to a specific external dependency, so a slow/failing dependency can't exhaust shared resources needed by unrelated parts of the application; (5) Fallbacks — provide a sensible default/cached response when the external call ultimately fails, if a degraded experience is acceptable, rather than failing the entire user-facing request.\n\nExample:\n// Using Resilience4j to combine timeout, retry, and circuit breaker\nCircuitBreakerConfig cbConfig = CircuitBreakerConfig.custom()\n    .failureRateThreshold(50) // open the circuit if 50% of recent calls fail\n    .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30))\n    .build();\nCircuitBreaker circuitBreaker = CircuitBreaker.of(\"externalApi\", cbConfig);\n\nSupplier<String> decoratedSupplier = CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(circuitBreaker,\n    () -> externalApiClient.fetchData());\nString result = Try.ofSupplier(decoratedSupplier)\n    .recover(throwable -> \"fallback default value\") // graceful fallback if circuit is open or call fails\n    .get();\n\nInterview Tip:\nStart with the core definition or approach, then explain the important reasoning clearly. For this design / scenario question, connect the concept to the practical example already provided above.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nUnderstanding of Core Java, ability to explain the concept clearly, and ability to connect the answer with practical Java development.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\n- Know the definition and the main purpose of the concept.\n- Be able to explain how it works in simple terms.\n- Understand the important limitation, trade-off, or edge case.\n- Be ready to explain the existing example step by step.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\n- Why is this approach used?\n- What are its advantages and limitations?\n- What happens in an edge case?\n- How would you implement or handle this in a real application?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Giving only a one-line definition without explaining the reasoning.\n- Memorizing the answer without understanding the example.\n- Ignoring important limitations or edge cases.\n- Using terminology without being able to explain it.\n\nDifficulty:\nIntermediate / Advanced\n\nInterview Focus:\nDesign / Scenario\n\nBest Answer Strategy:\nGive the main answer first, explain the reasoning in a logical sequence, and then use the provided example to demonstrate your understanding. If the interviewer asks a follow-up, answer the specific point directly rather than repeating the complete definition.",
